{"name":"Bagpiper","hex":"#1c5544","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bagpiper","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who plays the bagpipes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bagpiper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bagpipes","blocks":[{"text":"The bagpipe is a woodwind instrument using enclosed reeds fed from a constant reservoir of air in the form of a bag. The Scottish Great Highland bagpipe is well known, but people have played bagpipes for centuries throughout large parts of Europe, North Africa, West Asia, around the Persian Gulf and northern parts of South Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Scotland_Independence_March%2C_28_March_2026_%2832%29.jpg/330px-Scotland_Independence_March%2C_28_March_2026_%2832%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bagpipes","wikidata":"Q8347"},{"title":"Bagpiper (whisky)","blocks":[{"text":"Bagpiper is a brand of Indian whisky, manufactured by United Spirits Ltd (USL), a subsidiary of Diageo. It was launched in October 1976. Bagpiper is sold in a square bottle with black and gold packaging design. The company describes the whisky's flavour as \"a light malty aroma and a hint of a smooth woody character, owing to the use of malt spirits specifically matured in pre-identified American oak casks\". It is similar to a blended whisky flavored with pot still malt whisky, but the neutral spirits used as base are distilled from molasses instead of grain."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/42/Bagpiper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bagpiper_(whisky)","wikidata":"Q4842091"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Cantiga_bagpipes_1.jpg/500px-Cantiga_bagpipes_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src"